.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt NOQTIS-SRC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm noqtis src
.Nd fetch the sources of a capsule's data record, and say if they changed
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm noqtis
.Cm src add
.Op Fl u Ar url | Fl -url Ar url
.Op Fl -portal Cm ckan | socrata | arcgis
.Op Fl -base Ar url
.Op Fl -id Ar id
.Op Fl -resource Ar id
.Op Fl -format Ar fmt
.Op Fl -layer Ar n
.Op Fl -mirror Ar url ...
.Op Fl -archived Ar url
.Op Fl -wayback
.Op Fl -synthetic
.Op Fl -max Ar bytes
.Op Fl -timeout Ar s
.Ar dir file
.Nm noqtis
.Cm src resolve
.Op Fl j | Fl -json
.Op Fl w | Fl -write
.Op Fl -wayback
.Ar dir
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm noqtis
.Cm src fetch
.Op Fl j | Fl -json
.Op Fl f | Fl -force
.Op Fl -max Ar bytes
.Op Fl -timeout Ar s
.Ar dir
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm noqtis
.Cm src refresh
.Op Fl j | Fl -json
.Op Fl w | Fl -write
.Op Fl -max Ar bytes
.Op Fl -timeout Ar s
.Ar dir
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm noqtis
.Cm src list
.Op Fl j | Fl -json
.Ar dir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each source of the data record of the capsule folder
.Ar dir
.Pq the Cm sources No of Xr noqtis-data 5
records a file's SHA-256 and size and where it came from: its
.Cm url ,
the portal and dataset it was found through
.Pq Cm resolver ,
copies with the same bytes
.Pq Cm mirrors
and an archived copy
.Pq Cm archived .
These commands fetch from those addresses.
Bytes are only ever taken for a file when their size and SHA-256 are
those recorded
.Pq Cm fetch
or when
.Fl -write
asks for them to be recorded
.Pq Cm refresh ;
nothing fetched passes for the file otherwise.
A changed record must be sealed again
.Pq Xr noqtis-seal 1 ,
as each command says.
.Bl -tag -width "refresh"
.It Cm add
records
.Ar file ,
a path in
.Ar dir ,
as a source.
With
.Fl -url ,
that is its address; with
.Fl -portal ,
the portal at
.Fl -base
is asked where its dataset's file is (below), and both the resolver and
the address it gives are recorded.
When
.Ar file
is not there yet it is fetched from that address first, and the
server's ETag and Last-Modified and the time are recorded with it; a file
already there is recorded as it is.
.Fl -mirror
(up to 32) and
.Fl -archived
record copies;
.Fl -wayback
asks the Wayback Machine for its copy of the address closest to now and
records it as
.Cm archived ,
with its time.
.Fl -synthetic
marks a generated stand-in, which has no address
.Pq Xr noqtis-data 1 , Cm data syn .
.It Cm resolve
asks the portal of each source with one where its file is now, and says
whether that is the recorded
.Cm url
.Pq Ql same
or not
.Pq Ql moved ;
.Fl w
records the new address.
With
.Fl -wayback
it asks the Wayback Machine for a copy of each source's
.Cm url
instead, and records it as
.Cm archived .
.It Cm fetch
restores each file that is missing: it tries the source's addresses in
order, its
.Cm url ,
its portal, each mirror, then its archived copy (the raw bytes of a
Wayback Machine copy), and writes the first answer whose size and
SHA-256 are those recorded.
Every address tried and why it failed is said on standard error; each
file gets a line saying which copy was used
.Pq Ql fetched No ... Ql from url , portal , mirror No or Ql archived ,
or that it was already there
.Pq Ql present ,
or that no address gave the recorded bytes
.Pq Ql unreachable .
A file there with other bytes is kept
.Pq Ql differs
unless
.Fl f
replaces it with a copy that checks.
A synthetic source is never fetched.
.It Cm refresh
fetches each source again from its
.Cm url ,
or from its portal when it has no url, and says whether it is
.Ql unchanged ,
.Ql changed
(with the old and the new SHA-256 and size) or
.Ql unreachable ;
a synthetic source is said as such and not fetched.
Mirrors and archived copies hold the recorded bytes, so they are not
asked.
Nothing is written unless
.Fl w :
then each changed file is replaced by the new bytes and its source
records them (size, SHA-256, time, ETag, Last-Modified, and the address
a portal gave), its archived copy and mirrors, which held the old bytes,
are dropped from the record, and each such change is said.
.It Cm list
prints each source, its size and SHA-256, and its addresses in the order
.Cm fetch
tries them;
.Fl j
prints the record's
.Cm sources
as they are.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Ar file
arguments
.Cm resolve , fetch
and
.Cm refresh
act on every source; each
.Ar file
named must be one.
.Fl j
prints one JSON object: the folder, and per source its
.Cm path , status ,
.Cm synthetic
when it is, where it was fetched
.Pq Cm from , url
and a
.Cm detail ;
for a changed source
.Cm refresh
adds
.Cm old
and
.Cm new ,
each with
.Cm sha256
and
.Cm size ,
and ends with
.Cm changed , unreachable
and
.Cm written .
.Ss Portals
.Bl -tag -width "socrata"
.It Cm ckan
.Fl -base
is the portal (such as
.Ql https://data.ontario.ca ) ,
.Fl -id
the dataset's name or id: its
.Ql package_show
lists the resources, and the file is the one whose id or name is
.Fl -resource ,
else the first in
.Fl -format
(CSV when not given).
With
.Fl -resource
alone, a resource id, its
.Ql resource_show
names the file.
.It Cm socrata
.Fl -base
is the portal (such as
.Ql https://data.cityofchicago.org ) ,
.Fl -id
the dataset's four-by-four id; the file is its export of every row, CSV
or with
.Fl -format Cm json
JSON.
.It Cm arcgis
with
.Fl -id ,
an item id, of the portal
.Fl -base
(such as
.Ql https://www.arcgis.com ) :
a feature service item gives the query of every feature of its layer
.Fl -layer
(0 when not given) as GeoJSON
.Pf ( Fl -format Cm json :
Esri JSON), any other item its data.
Without
.Fl -id ,
.Fl -base
is a FeatureServer or MapServer and
.Fl -layer
its layer.
A server returns at most its record limit of features to one query.
.El
.Pp
.Xr nqdaddr 3
gives each request and how its reply is read.
An address a portal answers with must be http:// or https://, and
https:// when the portal is.
.Ss The network
The requests are HTTP/1.1 GETs, over https:// with the tool's TLS 1.3
client
.Pq Xr noqtis-check 1 No says which trust anchors it uses .
At most 5 redirects are followed, each to the same server (or from http
on port 80 to https on 443) and never from https:// to http://; a
redirect elsewhere is refused and says where, so that address can be
given instead.
A transfer cut short is resumed with a range request, up to 3 times,
when the server takes them and names the file by an ETag or
Last-Modified (an If-Range); otherwise it fails.
A file may be at most
.Fl -max
bytes (1 GiB when not given; with
.Cm fetch ,
its recorded size), and each takes at most
.Fl -timeout
seconds (600), with at most 30 seconds' wait for any read; a portal's
reply may be at most 16 MiB and take 120 seconds.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when every file was there or fetched
.Pq Cm refresh : No every source unchanged ;
1 when a file could not be fetched with its recorded bytes, or
.Pq Cm refresh
a source changed;
3 when
.Cm refresh
found nothing changed but a source unreachable;
2 on a usage, file or network error of
.Cm add .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Record a CSV file of an open-data portal, restore it later, and see
whether the portal's copy changed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
noqtis data init results
noqtis src add --portal ckan --base https://data.ontario.ca \e
  --id ontario-public-library-statistics --wayback \e
  results data/libraries.csv
noqtis seal -k key results
noqtis src fetch results
noqtis src refresh results
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-data 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-drift 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-seal 1 ,
.Xr nqdaddr 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-data 5
